<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">James Ensor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1860">1860</a>–<a href="/years/1949">1949</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">James Ensor was a Belgian painter and printmaker from Ostend, a forerunner of expressionism. He was a member of the avant-garde group Les XX, and his works, filled with masks, skeletons and crowds, mocked bourgeois society. His best-known painting, &quot;Christ's Entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1889&quot;, completed in 1888, is now in the J. Paul Getty Museum. He was made a baron in 1929.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-ensor.jpg" alt="James Ensor" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: James Ensor · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ensor_voor_zijn_schildersezel,_James_Ensor,_1890,_Koninklijk_Museum_voor_Schone_Kunsten_Antwerpen,_2809.001.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>James Sidney Edouard Ensor was born on 13 April 1860. His father, James Frederic Ensor, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> to English parents, and his mother, Maria Catherina Haegheman, was Belgian. He spent his childhood in a souvenir shop run by his parents and was fascinated by the carnival masks sold there, which became a source of inspiration. He left school at fifteen to train with two local painters, and from 1877 to 1880 he attended the Académie Royale des Beaux-Arts in Brussels. He first exhibited in 1881. From 1880 until 1917 he had his studio in the attic of his parents' house, and he travelled very little.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>His early works, such as Russian Music of 1881 and The Drunkards of 1883, show realistic scenes in a sombre style. His palette then brightened and his subjects became increasingly bizarre. Paintings such as The Scandalized Masks of 1883 and Skeletons Fighting over a Hanged Man of 1891 show figures in grotesque masks, and he dressed skeletons up in his studio. The years from 1888 to 1892 were a turning point, when he turned to religious themes, often the torments of Christ. Although he was an atheist, he identified with Christ as a victim of mockery.</p><p>In Christ's Entry into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a> in 1889 a vast carnival mob in grotesque masks advances toward the viewer, with Belgian politicians and members of Ensor's family among them and Christ on his donkey nearly lost in the throng. Les XX rejected it, and it was not publicly shown until 1929. Ensor also made 133 etchings and drypoints, 86 of them between 1886 and 1891. Late 19th-century critics often rejected his work as scandalous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Recognition came slowly. In 1895 the Royal Museums of Fine Arts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> acquired his painting The Lamp Boy, and he had his first solo exhibition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brussels">Brussels</a>. He was named a baron by King Albert in 1929 and received the Légion d'honneur in 1933. His output fell after the first decade of the 20th century, and historians have generally seen his last forty or fifty years as a period of decline. He turned increasingly to music and played the harmonium for visitors. He died in Ostend on 19 November 1949, aged 89.</p><p>He influenced expressionist and surrealist artists such as Paul Klee, Emil Nolde, George Grosz and Alfred Kubin. His work is in the Royal Museums of Fine Arts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, the Museum of Modern Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> and the Musée d'Orsay, among others.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “James Ensor” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Belgian culture has been prolific, from the painters <b>James Ensor</b> and René Magritte and the Art Nouveau of Victor Horta to the comics of Hergé, while Adolphe Sax invented the saxophone and Georges Lemaître proposed the theory of the expanding universe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ostend, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. Died in Ostend, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.23,2.92&amp;year=1917" aria-label="Ostend on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of James Ensor"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5156 5744h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5156 5744h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.23,2.92&amp;year=1917">What was here in 1917? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
